+
+Once a server is listed, `wizardingcode-mem sync` runs by itself: your agents' session-start and prompt hooks start it, it sends what the policy allows within seconds of a change, brings the team's entries and memories back, and stops after fifteen idle minutes. What arrives lives apart under `knowledge/received/<server>/`, read-only and marked with who wrote it; your agent sees it beside your own knowledge, never pinned, and at most a third of a brief's memory slots. `wizardingcode-mem knowledge take <server>:<slug>--<8 hex>` copies a received entry into your own repository to edit it. Several servers can be listed; a project binds to one, and the level `receive` binds it without sending anything. `wizardingcode-mem status` shows the sync and each server; `doctor` warns when something needs you; `share server remove` deletes everything a server left here (the device stays listed on the server until an admin revokes it). A restore, a store move and a compact stop the sync first; after a restore the withdrawals the reset levels would cause are held until you run `share server resume`.
